1
00:00:00,354 --> 00:00:03,197
The path up the mountain takes most of a day.

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:16,093
Loose stone underfoot, the air thinning with every switchback, until the fortress finally appears above the clouds like something that was never meant to be reached by ordinary means.

3
00:00:18,135 --> 00:00:31,510
No siege road, no easy approach, just one narrow track, climbing toward a gate that has swallowed thousands of recruits over the centuries, and revealed almost nothing about what happened to them once they passed through it.

4
00:00:32,912 --> 00:00:34,153
This is a Lamate.

5
00:00:34,974 --> 00:00:36,035
The Eagles nest.

6
00:00:37,797 --> 00:00:44,205
A fortress built into the Al-Bor's mountains of what is now northern Iran seized in 1090 by a man named Hassanai Sabah.

7
00:00:44,805 --> 00:00:50,311
Outsiders would later call him the old man of the mountain, a title that sounds almost mythical and for good reason.

8
00:00:50,852 --> 00:00:56,178
For nearly two centuries after he took the fortress, it trained some of the most feared men in the medieval world.

9
00:00:56,322 --> 00:00:59,345
and almost nobody outside its walls understood exactly how.

10
00:01:00,567 --> 00:01:07,074
King's hired armies, Sultans hired more soldiers than they could count, filled entire garrisons, built walls thick enough to withstand a siege lasting years.

11
00:01:07,514 --> 00:01:10,838
But when a Nazari is mildly leader wanted a rival removed, he didn't send an army at all.

12
00:01:11,279 --> 00:01:18,767
He sent one man sometimes armed until the final moment, into the heart of an enemy's own court, passed the guards, passed the walls, passed every precaution that wealth and power could buy.

13
00:01:20,008 --> 00:01:22,591
And the strangest part of this story isn't that it worked once.

14
00:01:24,173 --> 00:01:33,604
It's that it worked for, Generations against some of the most heavily protected men alive, across an empire that outnumbered the Nazaris at every possible turn.

15
00:01:34,945 --> 00:01:36,307
Stage 1 Selection

16
00:01:37,789 --> 00:01:39,812
Selection rarely began with strength.

17
00:01:41,213 --> 00:01:47,922
The men chosen to become Fediene, the self-sacrificing agents of the Nazari state, were rarely picked for size or swordsmanship.

18
00:01:48,258 --> 00:02:02,059
They were chosen young, often from displaced or vulnerable backgrounds, brought to a landmark by recruiters who had learned exactly what kind of person could be reshaped completely, not the strongest boy in the village, not the most skilled fighter with a blade, the one with nothing left to lose

19
00:02:02,219 --> 00:02:03,761
and nowhere else in the world to belong.

20
00:02:05,043 --> 00:02:09,650
That detail alone tells you something the popular legends usually leave out entirely.

21
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:14,838
But the legends are where this story starts to get genuinely strange.

22
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:17,842
Stage 2, the legend,

23
00:02:18,786 --> 00:02:28,821
This is the part almost everyone has heard some version of, and it comes from an account written decades later, by a man who almost certainly never set foot inside the fortress himself.

24
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:43,884
Marco Polo, the Venetian merchant, passed through the region long after Lamat's fall and described a hidden garden concealed somewhere inside the mountain, filled with wine, food and beautiful attendance, an entire paradise built in miniature and sealed away from the outside world.

25
00:02:44,565 --> 00:02:54,671
According to his account, young recruits were drugged into unconsciousness, carried into this garden while they slept and allowed to wake surrounded by every pleasure they had never known in their short, difficult lives.

26
00:02:55,972 --> 00:03:10,091
Then, after only a brief taste of it, drugged again and carried back out into ordinary life, told once they woke the second time that what they'd briefly experienced was only a glimpse, a preview of the true paradise waiting for them and only them if they died in the

27
00:03:10,151 --> 00:03:11,233
service of their master.

28
00:03:12,454 --> 00:03:15,378
It's one of the most repeated stories in medieval history.

29
00:03:15,842 --> 00:03:27,593
It shaped how an entire continent imagined this group for the better part of 700 years, echoing through travellers' tales, court records, and eventually popular fiction that still borrows the same imagery today.

30
00:03:28,874 --> 00:03:33,018
Most historians studying the Nazari state now believe it almost certainly.

31
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:35,280
Never happened.

32
00:03:37,202 --> 00:03:41,446
There's no evidence for a hidden garden anywhere in the archaeological record at a Lambert.

33
00:03:41,987 --> 00:03:56,822
No mention of it in any surviving contemporary Ismaili source Despite the fortress leaving behind a substantial, written legacy on other matters entirely, including theology, poetry, and internal governance, what historians do find again and again is a pattern.

34
00:03:58,104 --> 00:04:10,862
Enemies of the Nazaris and travellers who arrived generations after the fact with no direct access to the fortress at all needed some explanation for why young men would calmly walk toward a death sentence without hesitation or apparent fear.

35
00:04:11,603 --> 00:04:17,563
A drugged, manufactured paradise was simply easier to believe than genuine religious and political conviction.

36
00:04:19,265 --> 00:04:27,576
It made the frightening comprehensible, and it made outsiders feel less unsettled by something they didn't understand and couldn't easily replicate themselves.

37
00:04:28,897 --> 00:04:31,200
Which raises an uncomfortable question.

38
00:04:33,123 --> 00:04:41,714
If the garden was never real, what actually turned a frightened recruit into someone willing to die on command without ever needing to be drugged into believing it.

39
00:04:42,658 --> 00:04:44,040
Stage three, training.

40
00:04:45,542 --> 00:04:48,206
Training had almost nothing to do with combat at all.

41
00:04:49,588 --> 00:04:58,199
Fedain spent years learning languages, regional customs, and court etiquette, sometimes entire religious traditions completely different from the one they'd been raised in.

42
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:11,698
They studied how a merchant walked through a crowded market, how a scholar spoke in front of an audience, how a soldier from a rival army carried himself while standing guard at a gate, because the goal was never to overpower an enemy's defenses through brute force.

43
00:05:12,738 --> 00:05:22,151
It was to become someone an enemy's own guards would never think to search in the first place, someone who belonged in a room simply because nobody could imagine why he shouldn't.

44
00:05:23,393 --> 00:05:29,121
This kind of preparation could stretch on for years, quietly, without a single mission ever being assigned.

45
00:05:29,782 --> 00:05:35,650
A recruit might master an entire second identity before he ever set foot outside the mountains that trained him.

46
00:05:36,872 --> 00:05:38,174
Stage 4, doctrine.

47
00:05:39,055 --> 00:05:41,298
This is where the real transformation happened.

48
00:05:41,538 --> 00:05:45,466
not in a hidden garden, in years of patient, deliberate instruction.

49
00:05:46,789 --> 00:05:56,990
The Nazari Ismailis were a minority sect within a minority sect, surrounded on every side by a Sunni Seljuk empire that vastly outnumbered them in soldiers, territory and wealth.

50
00:05:57,711 --> 00:06:02,802
Assassination wasn't chosen because it was easy, or because it satisfied some appetite for violence.

51
00:06:03,234 --> 00:06:11,506
It was chosen because it was one of the only forms of warfare available to a state that could never hope to win an open battlefield fight against enemies many times its size.

52
00:06:12,888 --> 00:06:19,278
Every fedain was taught this directly, repeatedly, until it became the foundation of how he understood his own life.

53
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:22,583
He wasn't a hired killer working for money.

54
00:06:24,286 --> 00:06:34,732
He was a soldier in an army too small and too outnumbered to fight any other way, defending a faith and a homeland that had no other means of defense against empires, that could field ten times its number of men.

55
00:06:35,873 --> 00:06:37,395
Stage five, infiltration.

56
00:06:38,616 --> 00:06:41,579
It could take years before it ever produced a single result.

57
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:53,651
Fedain might spend months, sometimes years, working quietly inside an enemy's court as a servant, a merchant or a soldier, waiting patiently for trust to be earned, and for the right opportunity to finally present itself.

58
00:06:53,671 --> 00:07:00,498
He might rise slowly through minor positions, take on small responsibilities, become a familiar face nobody thought twice about seeing in the hallway.

59
00:07:00,738 --> 00:07:03,841
patience wasn't treated as an incidental virtue in this line of work.

60
00:07:04,962 --> 00:07:11,909
It was the entire method, the single most important skill above every other, more valued than strength or speed with a blade.

61
00:07:13,170 --> 00:07:14,531
Stage 6 The Strike

62
00:07:15,812 --> 00:07:17,935
It was almost never meant to be secret.

63
00:07:19,296 --> 00:07:35,967
In 1092, the powerful Seljuk Vizier Nizarm Almalk, one of the most influential statesmen in the entire Islamic world at the time, and the architect of much of the Emperor's administration, was killed by a fed Iain disguised as a wandering Sufi mystic, stabbed while being carried through camp in

64
00:07:36,007 --> 00:07:38,330
his litter at dusk.

65
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:39,812
A century later, in 1192,

66
00:07:40,814 --> 00:07:55,015
Conrad of Montferat, the newly elected King of Jerusalem, was assassinated in broad daylight on a public street, stabbed by two men who had spent months quietly embedding themselves inside his court beforehand, waiting for exactly this moment to arrive.

67
00:07:56,176 --> 00:08:00,082
Both killings happened in public, both were meant to be witnessed by as many people as possible.

68
00:08:00,194 --> 00:08:02,436
because the message was never aimed only at the man who died.

69
00:08:02,837 --> 00:08:12,386
It was aimed at every other powerful figure watching from what they assumed was a safe distance, telling them plainly and unmistakably that no fortress, no bodyguard, and no amount of distance could ever fully guarantee their safety.

70
00:08:13,267 --> 00:08:14,468
Stage 7, The Ending.

71
00:08:15,549 --> 00:08:20,294
It eventually came for both the individual assassin and, generations later, for the entire order itself.

72
00:08:21,315 --> 00:08:24,318
Most Fedain made no attempt to escape after completing a mission.

73
00:08:24,338 --> 00:08:28,402
Capture and the execution that inevitably followed wasn't treated as a failure of the plan.

74
00:08:28,578 --> 00:08:36,205
It was treated as the expected final chapter, a kind of martyrdom built directly into the role from the very beginning of training, long before a single target had ever been chosen.

75
00:08:37,066 --> 00:08:48,998
For over a century and a half, this pattern held, spreading a very specific and very effective kind of fear across an empire, the Nazaris could never have defeated through open war, no matter how long the conflict dragged on or how many soldiers were sent against them.

76
00:08:49,898 --> 00:08:57,906
Then in 1256, the Mongol leader Hilargukhan arrived at Alamat with a force the Nazaris had no real answer for, one that dwarfed anything the fortress had ever faced before.

77
00:08:58,050 --> 00:09:05,117
The stronghold that had never fallen in nearly 200 years that had outlasted Sultans, Crusader kings, and entire rival dynasties, surrendered within days.

78
00:09:06,058 --> 00:09:14,066
Its library said to hold irreplaceable records of the sex history, philosophy, and closely guarded secrets, was burned to the ground, most of it lost forever to later historians.

79
00:09:14,567 --> 00:09:22,355
The order that had terrified Sultans and kings for generations ended not with a hidden dagger in a crowded street, but with an army simply too large and too overwhelming to outmaneuver.

80
00:09:23,676 --> 00:09:25,618
Which brings this back to that path up the mountain.

81
00:09:25,826 --> 00:09:35,941
A recruit climbing toward a fortress that no longer exists, toward a story that has somehow survived far longer than the state that originally created it, retold and reshaped by every generation that found it too strange to leave alone.

82
00:09:36,863 --> 00:09:41,430
Most of what people think they know about the assassins isn't exactly a lie, it's something stranger than that.

83
00:09:42,852 --> 00:09:51,565
A legend, invented by outsiders who couldn't otherwise explain the loyalty they were witnessing firsthand, that ended up out living the far more complicated truth, it was quietly built to replace.

84
00:09:52,607 --> 00:09:54,610
The garden was never real,

85
00:09:56,898 --> 00:10:12,545
The men who climbed that mountain and the actual reasons they were willing to die once they finally reached the top were something far more human, shaped by conviction, patience and years of belonging to something larger than themselves, and somehow in the end.

86
00:10:13,908 --> 00:10:17,554
Far more unsettling than any drugged paradise could ever have been.
